          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Guidance for type 2 diabetes describes two main kinds of activity, plus a note about sitting.\n\n**Aerobic activity:** The guidance in the *Physical activity for type 2 diabetes* summary is at least 150 minutes per week of moderate-to-vigorous aerobic activity. It should be spread over at least three days, with no more than two days in a row without activity. The summary says the benefit to blood sugar after a session lasts about 24 to 72 hours, so spreading sessions across the week works better than doing it all on one weekend. Moderate activity means breathing harder but still being able to talk. Brisk walking, cycling and swimming are examples. The *Physical activity for high blood pressure* summary gives the same general figures.\n\n**Resistance training:** The diabetes summary recommends resistance training on two to three non-consecutive days a week.\n\n**Sitting:** The retrieved passages do not cover sitting specifically for diabetes. The blood pressure summary says long periods of uninterrupted sitting are linked to worse heart outcomes, partly separately from exercise levels. It describes breaking up sitting as a separate goal, not a replacement for weekly activity.\n\n**Safety points:** The diabetes summary lists conditions that change how activity should be approached. These include:\n- Possible heart disease, where a check before vigorous activity may be advised.\n- Eye damage (retinopathy), where high-impact or straining activity may be restricted.\n- Nerve damage in the feet, where daily foot checks and suitable footwear matter, and low-impact options such as cycling or swimming may be preferred.\n- Nerve damage affecting heart rate, where heart rate is unreliable as an effort guide, so how hard the activity feels is used instead.\n- Very high blood sugar with ketones, where activity should wait until this is corrected.\n\nThe retrieved text of this safety section is partly cut off, so it may not be complete.\n\nStarting below the target and building up gradually tends to work better than starting at full level. Before starting a new routine, it is worth discussing with your clinician which of these points apply to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
